we've all heard the narrative apple is  too big amazon is too big google is too  big it seems like these tech giants have  formed unbreakable monopolies around  various segments of the market and that  they're quote-unquote too big to fail  but if history has taught us anything  it's that no company is too big to fail  sure the government and stockholders can  bail out the failing company and give it  another 10 20 30 years of lifetime but  you can only do this for so long and  eventually these companies will fail as  well  just take a look at the biggest  companies throughout history sears  roebuck and co for example is often  credited with inventing the department  store and throughout the 1900s they were  the biggest retailer in the entire world  yet in 2018 sears filed for bankruptcy  and shut down all of their stores  similarly chrysler and general motors  were two of the biggest names in the  automotive industry throughout the 1900s  yet in the late 2000s both of them filed  for bankruptcy now of course these guys  got bailed out because they were  integral to the automotive industry in  the late 2000s but do you think they'll  be bailed out again if they miss out on  the eevee transition  if there's a handful of eb companies  like tesla rivian and maybe even ford  overshadowing them i don't think they'll  be bailed out also if these companies  were truly too big to fill they would  have never needed a bailout in the first  place so clearly all companies come and  go but how does a company go from being  an unstoppable force in the market to  eventually being forgotten about  the first struggle that every large  corporation has to deal with is the  pains of scale we always hear about  economies of scale and i'm sure you guys  know exactly what this is economies of  scale is a cost advantage that large  companies enjoy due to serving a large  market pains of skill or diseconomies of  skill on the other hand are a cost  disadvantage that comes along with being  too big  you see small companies don't have human  resources or fancy corporate events and  they don't fly around executives and  directors around the world  their employees also don't spend hours  every day answering emails or  participating in meetings in other words  smaller companies generally have little  to no operational expenses youtubers are  a great example of this  while there are some creators like mr  beast and good mythical morning with  massive teams and production expenses  behind them most creators have extremely  small operational expenses pewdiepie  wolf athlete next graham stefan andrei  [ __ ] and mark rober are all basically  just one man shows sure they may have a  few employees to help them edit and make  sponsorship deals and so on but compared  to their income the amount they spend on  payroll is extremely small not to  mention it most of these guys just  record in their bedroom or a small set  inside their house  so if they have a bad month in terms of  views it doesn't really matter they  won't make as much money but they're  also not in any risk of losing money the  same however cannot be said about a  channel like the infographic show if you  were to replicate one of their videos  using professional talent it'll easily  cost you 10 thousand dollars per video  but i'm sure they have insane economies  of skill maybe their cost per video is  only three thousand dollars but to get  to that number they had to take on a  boatload of fixed expenses in the last  month for example the infographic show  posted 42 videos and even if each video  only cost them three thousand dollars  we're talking about a fixed cost of  about 126 000 per month given how many  views the infographic show gets every  month i'm sure they pull in way more  than this in terms of revenue but with  that being said their operational  expenses are a much bigger percentage of  their revenue than someone like andrei  jig this means that it would be  significantly easier for andre to wear  their 50 percent down turn it than it  would be for the infographic show this  same principle also applies to the  biggest companies in the world  regardless of how the next iphone  performs apple has to pay tens of  millions to keep the ufo and california  alive they have to pay for tens of  thousands of man-hours spent in meetings  and answering emails and they have to  pay large salaries to thousands of  managers and directors that a small  corporation simply wouldn't need while  these may seem like negligible expenses  compared to apple's profits the thing to  note is that all these factors create  friction and this friction makes apple  slower and slower which gives up and  coming competitors the perfect  opportunity to overtake them  aside from being a slower behemoth  larger companies usually fall into the  trap of prioritizing profits over  innovation  this isn't always the company's fault  per se  the problem is often that they've simply  hit a dead end within their industry  i mean honestly how much better can you  make an iphone or a macbook really all  that's left is consistently improving  the speed and reliability of these  devices year after year and as each  generational jump becomes a smaller and  smaller difference people upgrade less  often which forces these companies to  look for alternative modes of revenue to  continue growth now apple has done a  phenomenal job with all their services  whether that be icloud or apple music  but none of these are new inventions or  are particularly innovative the same  thing can be said about amazon a lot of  logistics innovation had to be done to  get delivery time frames down from one  week to two days to next day to same day  to one hour delivery but how much more  could amazon really push this and how  much more would consumers even care  about that improvement the truth is that  apple has solved the smartphone problem  and amazon has solved the logistics  problem the only step left is to refine  and maintain as long as possible but the  problem is that the top talent is not  interested in refining and maintaining  do you really think the top engineers  are interested in making the iphone  match smaller or removing the camera  bump  no the top engineers are interested in  developing artificial intelligence  leveraging machine learning programming  self-driving cars creating the metaverse  launching rockets and so on and so forth  so as larger companies start to  prioritize profits over innovation the  top talent will slowly bleed into  up-and-coming companies in the meantime  the largest companies are left with nba  businessmen who are generally more  interested in progressing themselves  than progressing the company now i'm not  faulting these guys for prioritizing  self-preservation over pushing forward  the company but whether their reasoning  is justified or not in the end the  company is hurt and this leads to an  extremely dangerous negative feedback  loop as employees care less and less  about the future of the company their  products become less and less inspiring  which attracts worse and worse talent  which then makes their products even  less inspiring if this sustains for long  enough you'll end up like jcpenney wells  fargo atp dish ge etc if we're being  honest none of the employees at these  companies give a crap about the future  of the company they're all there for one  reason and one reason only a paycheck  and when a company gets to this point  it's all but over  when all the innovation within an  industry is gone what follows next is  always a race to the bottom in the  automotive industry for example once  most of the innovation was done toyota  and honda stepped in and made cars  cheaper and more affordable than ever  before similarly once the airline  deregulation act of 1978 passed airline  ticket prices fell off a cliff this is  quite natural as innovation slows down a  given product or service loses much of  its appeal and companies fan bases  dissipate this isn't to say that people  no longer use said product or service  but simply that they no longer take  pride in a certain brand for example in  the late 1800s a wealthy gentleman may  have been proud to get their electricity  from the best provider in the nation  today electricity is used more than ever  before but no one gives a crap about who  the electricity provider is electricity  is just a part of daily life and the  people choose to provide her based on  who's the cheapest not where brand it's  from similarly when was the last time  you cared about where your gas comes  from or where your steel comes from  even if you do care about these factors  it's likely because you care about the  product itself as opposed to the company  maybe you only buy your steel and gas  from one company because you need a  certain quality of steel or you want the  highest quality of gas but in these  scenarios you're not loyal to the brand  you're loyal to the product if a new gas  company came out tomorrow and offered a  better quality of gas for the same price  you'd likely switch in a heartbeat  clearly the same thing cannot be said  about iphones and androids or mac os and  windows right now  these inventions are still rather new  and the people are still extremely  divided on these subjects but with time  people will care less and less as these  products basically become the same thing  something else to note is that many of  these companies have already experienced  this in the past apple for example  nearly went to bankrupt in the late  1990s after the initial pc boom came to  an end and the budget options flooded  the market they had to pivot to the  mobile market to reignite growth  similarly microsoft had a decade of  stagnation after 2000 as they had  already won the pc market it wasn't  until microsoft embraced the cloud and  servers they started to grow once again  if microsoft and apple hadn't made these  shifts it's likely that they would have  already gone bankrupt or ended up like  ibm  i know a lot of people would like to see  amazon go bankrupt but what's ironic is  that the person who is most confident  that amazon will go bankrupt is jeff  bezos in 2018 jeff said quote amazon is  not too big to fail  in fact i predict one day amazon will  fail amazon will go bankrupt if you look  at large companies their lifespans tend  to be 30 plus years not a hundred plus  years and if we think about it many of  the big tech companies that people  complain about aren't even 30 years old  amazon google tesla nvidia and facebook  are all less than 30 years old the two  big tech companies that are older than  that are apple and microsoft and as we  previously touched on it the only reason  these guys are still crushing it is  because they basically became a new  business right around that 30-year mark  meanwhile amazon and google are fast  approaching that 30-year mark and  likewise they've basically reached  market saturation in their main  industries and they're facing more  backlash than ever before similarly  facebook may be the most hated company  in the world and they haven't even been  around for 20 years this isn't to say  that any of these companies are gonna  collapse anytime soon but this next  decade could very well be the largest  and most dominant that you'll ever see  them after all it's just a matter of  time until a company's friction builds  up they start to prioritize profits over  innovation they lose their top talent  cheaper alternatives enter the market  the customer base dissipates and the  company becomes a shell of their former  self and i think jeff bezos says it best  companies have short life spans charlie  and amazon will be disrupted one day and  you worry about that i don't worry about  it because i know it's inevitable  companies come and go  and the companies that are the shiniest  and  most important of any era you wait a few  decades and they're gone and your job is  to make sure that you delay that date  i'd sure i would love for it to be after  i'm dead  [Laughter]  when do you guys think apple will file  for bankruptcy comment that down below  also drop a like if you hate bailouts  and of course consider checking out our  international channels to watch our  videos in other languages and consider  subscribing to see more questions that  logically answered but until then i'm  hari and i'll see you guys on the next  one
 

